% ============================================================================
% heat-transfer-example — for each of the three heat-transfer modes, the
% everyday EXAMPLE the SAME already-cited NASA sentence gives (adj-facts-
% stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `heat-transfer.adj`
% (`heat_transfer_mode(mode, mechanism)`, HOW / through-what each mode
% moves heat — conduction → direct_contact, convection →
% motion_of_gasses_and_liquids, radiation → light_waves). That table's own
% header already quotes, verbatim, the NASA sentence that states each
% mechanism, and the SAME sentence closes with a parenthetical `(e.g.,
% ...)` everyday example — a fact the mechanism-only schema had no room
% for:
%
%   conduction → hot_chocolate_cup
%     "Conduction - the transfer of thermal energy by coming in direct
%      contact with another object (e.g., holding a cup of hot chocolate,
%      walking bare foot across a cold floor)"
%
%   convection → warm_air_rising
%     "Convection - the motion of gasses and liquids due to different
%      densities (e.g., when warm air rises in your home)"
%
%   radiation → sunlight_on_skin
%     "Radiation - the absorption and emitting of visible or invisible
%      light waves, called radiation, to from one object to another (e.g.,
%      sunlight on your skin, heating food in a microwave)"
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? heat_transfer_example(conduction, $Example)   % hot_chocolate_cup
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `heat-transfer.adj`'s own `heat_transfer_mode`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `heat_transfer_example(mode, example)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the example WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of the three modes. All
% three modes have an example in this SAME cited span, so this sibling
% covers the full three-mode domain with NO abstention among the three.
% (Conduction and radiation each list a SECOND example — a cold floor, and
% microwaving food — sitting unused for a possible future alt-example
% sibling; convection lists only the one.) The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind an example and recall which mode it illustrates:
%
%     ? heat_transfer_example($Mode, warm_air_rising)   % convection
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME three NASA sentences
% already quoted inside `heat-transfer.adj`'s own header, from the SAME
% NASA Next Gen STEM "Thermal Energy Transfer" Educator Notes that table
% already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the
% example half of each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE
% provenance envelope (per `heat-transfer.adj`'s own convention), so
% `source`/`locator`/`trust` below hold the conduction span; the other two
% rows' own spans are listed above for independent auditability. `trust
% authoritative` — the same tier `heat-transfer.adj` already carries (a
% primary U.S. government NASA education page).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table heat_transfer_example {
    columns mode, example

    row (conduction, hot_chocolate_cup)         % NASA: "Conduction - the transfer of thermal energy by coming in direct contact with another object (e.g., holding a cup of hot chocolate, walking bare foot across a cold floor)"
    row (convection, warm_air_rising)           % NASA: "Convection - the motion of gasses and liquids due to different densities (e.g., when warm air rises in your home)"
    row (radiation, sunlight_on_skin)           % NASA: "Radiation - the absorption and emitting of visible or invisible light waves, called radiation, to from one object to another (e.g., sunlight on your skin, heating food in a microwave)"

    source "Conduction - the transfer of thermal energy by coming in direct contact with another object (e.g., holding a cup of hot chocolate, walking bare foot across a cold floor)"
    locator "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/thermal-energy-transfer-classroom-connection-final-v508.pdf"
    trust authoritative

    cites "Convection - the motion of gasses and liquids due to different densities (e.g., when warm air rises in your home)" locator "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/thermal-energy-transfer-classroom-connection-final-v508.pdf"
    cites "Radiation - the absorption and emitting of visible or invisible light waves, called radiation, to from one object to another (e.g., sunlight on your skin, heating food in a microwave)" locator "https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/thermal-energy-transfer-classroom-connection-final-v508.pdf"
}
